---
title: "BOM's On Target: Assessing the Beaureau's Forecast Accuracy"
author: 'Greg Foletta'
date: '2025-06-14'
categories: [R Forecasting Weather]
---

In this article I'm going to take a look at how accurate Australia's Bureau of Metorology (BOM) is at forcasting temperature. But before I start a quick note.

I had trepidation writing this article, and it almost didn't get off the ground. All too often people wander into fields they have no foundational knowledge in, and armed with data and start to make bold statements and inferences. I'm not a meteorologist, and simply put I didn't want to be one of these people. But after having a chat with one of my great friends who has a Phd in meteorology, she assuaged my apprehension, and so here we are.

THe premise of this article came about after talking to people about the weather, often with them complaining that the forecasts were bad. I didn't necessarily believe that and went looking for historical data on how the BOM has performed, but couldn't find any. So what we'll take a look at in this article is how well the BOM has done at forecasting the temperature from 1 to 7 days out.


```{r include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(echo = FALSE)

library(tidyverse)
library(httr2)
library(rvest)
library(janitor)
library(glue)
library(tictoc)
library(here)
library(gt)
library(plotly)
library(gganimate)
library(cmdstanr)
library(tidybayes)

library(rnaturalearth)
library(rnaturalearthdata)

scale_colour_brewer_d <- function(..., palette = "Dark2") {
  scale_colour_brewer(..., palette = palette )
}

scale_fill_brewer_d <- function(..., palette = "Dark2") {
  scale_fill_brewer(..., palette = palette)
}

options(
  ggplot2.discrete.colour = scale_colour_brewer_d,
  ggplot2.discrete.fill = scale_fill_brewer_d
)
```

# Weather Stations

In previous articles I've gone into detail about how I got the data I'm working wth. This is probably because it's the most enjoyable part of the whole process. This time however I'm going to keep it brief and give you a quick overview about how I got the data, and what the data is.

I needed three pieces of data:

1. A list of BOM weather stations
1. The temperature at those weather stations over a period of time
1. The one to seven day forecast at those stations


Number one was easy, as the BOM provides a [list of weather stattions](http://www.bom.gov.au/climate/data/lists_by_element/stations.txt), including their name, latitude, longitude. A shout out here to the [read_fwf()](https://readr.tidyverse.org/reference/read_fwf.html) function which makes reading in this human-readable style text table easy. This list get's filtered down from ~6,500 total active weather stations to ~900 that have a world meteorological organisation ID. Here's a map of all of these weather stations around Australia:

```{r include=FALSE}
temp_data <- readRDS('bom_temperature.Rdata') |> drop_na(temperature)

forecast_distinct <- 
    readRDS('bom_forecasts_change.Rdata') |> 
    drop_na(temperature) |>
    filter(forecast_duration > 0)

forecast_mapping <-
  tibble(
    forecast_duration = 60*60*24*c(1:6),
    forecast_duration_names = as_factor(glue_data(list(a = 1:6), "{a} day"))
)
forecast_lagged <- readRDS('bom_forecasts_day_lags.Rdata') |> 
  drop_na(temperature) |>
  left_join(forecast_mapping, by = 'forecast_duration')

weather_stations <- 
    read_csv('weather_stations_current_data.csv', show_col_types = FALSE) |> 
    separate(location, into = c('city', 'state'), sep = ', ', remove = FALSE)
```

```{r weather_stations, eval=FALSE, include=FALSE}
# We keep this in here for posterity, but we get the weather station data in via csv that was generated from this.
weather_stations_raw <-
    request('http://www.bom.gov.au/climate/data/lists_by_element/stations.txt') |>
    req_user_agent('Mozilla') |>
    req_perform() |>
    resp_body_string() 

weather_stations <-
    weather_stations_raw |> 
    read_fwf(skip = 3, show_col_types = FALSE) |>  
    rename(
        site = X1, dist = X2, site_name = X3, start = X4,
        end = X5, lat = X6, lon = X7, source = X8,
        state = X9, height = X10, bar_ht = X11, wmo = X12
    ) |>
    slice_head(n = -6) 

nearest_location <- function(loc_html) {
        tibble(
            location = html_element(loc_html, 'a') |> html_text(),
            path = html_element(loc_html, 'a') |> html_attr('href'),
            loc_lat = html_element(loc_html, 'li') |> html_attr('data-lat'),
            loc_lon = html_element(loc_html, 'li') |> html_attr('data-lon')
        )
}

weather_stations_location <-
    weather_stations |>
    slice_tail(n = -1) |> 
    filter(wmo != '..') |> 
    mutate(reqs = map2(lat, lon, ~{ glue('http://www.bom.gov.au/places/search/?q={.x},{.y}&suggest=1') |> request() |> req_user_agent('Mozilla') })) |>
    mutate(nearest_location = req_perform_parallel(reqs) |> resps_successes() |> resps_data(resp_data = \(resp)(list(nearest_location(resp_body_html(resp)))))) |>
    select(-reqs) |> 
    unnest(nearest_location)
```
```{r echo=FALSE}
aus <- ne_countries(returnclass = "sf", scale = 'medium', country = 'Australia')
aus |>
    ggplot() +
    geom_sf() +
    geom_point(data = weather_stations, aes(x = lon, y = lat, colour = state), size = .2) +
    coord_sf(xlim = c(114, 153), ylim = -c(11, 43)) +
    labs(
        title = 'WMO Weather Station Locations in Australia',
        x = 'Lon',
        y = 'Lat',
        colour = 'State'
    )
```

# Temperature Data

To acquire the temperature and forecast data, I wrote a script which reaches out to the BOM and retrieves the temperature and the forecasts for the clostest city/town to each of the weather stations. This was wrapped up into a systemd service and timer and ran it every ten minutes, which appears to be the update interval for most temperature on the BOM website.

After some post-processing and cleaning, including a bit of a nightmare with timezones and an annoying daylight savings change during the sampling, the actual temperatures and the forecast temperatures are joined together. I then calculate the forecast period, which is the time between the time of the request and the time of the forecast. Here's a view of the temperature of each location over time, coloured by state:  

```{r eval=FALSE, include=FALSE}
set.seed(233433)
forecast_lagged |>
    group_by(wmo) |>
    nest(.key = 'data') |>
    ungroup() |> 
    slice_sample(n = 2) |>
    unnest(data) |>
    group_by(city, state) |>
    slice_head(n = 4) |>
    select(request_datetime_local_floored, wmo, city, state, forecast_datetime_local, forecast_duration, forecast_temp, temperature) |>
    gt() |> 
    cols_label(
  
              request_datetime_local_floored = "Request Date/Time",
        wmo = 'WMO ID',
        forecast_datetime_local = 'Forecast Date/Time',
        forecast_duration = 'Forecast Duration',
        temperature = 'Recorded Temperature'
    )
```
```{r echo=FALSE}
temp_data |>
    ggplot() +
    geom_point(aes(temp_datetime, temperature, colour = state), size = .01, alpha = .05) +
    guides(colour = guide_legend(override.aes = list(size = 2, alpha = 1))) +
    labs(
        title = "Australian Weather Station - Temperature over Time",
        x = 'Date/Time',
        y = 'Temperature (°C)',
        colour = 'State'
    ) +
    facet_wrap(vars(state))
```
That's a total of `r temp_data |> nrow()` million temperature readings over `r temp_data |> summarise(duration = last(request_datetime_utc - first(request_datetime_utc))) |> pull(duration) |> as.duration()`. It's not the the cleanest visualisation, but gives you a sense of the data we're working with, and as well the breadth of temperature difference in this vast country. If you squint you should be able to a general downward trend of the temperature as we move through Autumn towards Winter.

I could easily go off on a number of tangents with this data, but I'll just pull out the locations with the highest and lowest mean temperatures during this period:
```{r echo=FALSE}
# Calculate the mean temperature per station ID, then slice the stations with
# the highest and lowest mean temperature, then graph these
temp_data |>
    group_by(station_id) |>
    summarise(
        mean_temp = mean(temperature, na.rm = TRUE)
    ) |>
    ungroup() |>
    (\(x)
        bind_rows(
            slice_min(x, n = 1, mean_temp),
            slice_max(x, n = 1, mean_temp)
        )
    )() |>
    left_join(temp_data, by = 'station_id') |>
    ggplot() +
    geom_line(aes(request_datetime_utc_floored, temperature, colour = paste(city, state, sep = ', ')), linewidth = .4) +
    geom_hline(aes(yintercept = mean_temp, colour = paste(city, state, sep = ', '))) +
    labs(
        colour = 'City, State',
        x = 'Date/Time',
        y = 'Temperature'
    )  +
    guides(colour = guide_legend(override.aes = list(size = 2, alpha = 1)))
```
Unsurpirisingly we've got a city in the north of the country, and a mountain in the very south. This offers up an opportunity to disclose a problem with the data. If you look at the lows in Kunanyi/Mount Wellington between April 1st and 15th, you'll see their lower bound is 0. I forgot to account for the '-' in my data acquisition script! In a more rigorous analysis I'd probably model and impute these negative temperatures for this and other sites. But taking a look, there doesn't appear to be many temperatures that would have been below zero, and the sheer weight of other data means these outliers shouldn't have a significant effect on what I'm looking at.

# Forecast Data

The BOM provides a detailed 3-hourly forecst for each location (1:00AM, 4:00AM, ... 10:00PM) out for 7 days, and this is what we acquire from their website every ten minutes for each location. This means we get 8 (hourly forecasts) * 7 (days) * 526 (locations) = 29,456 forecasts every ten minutes, most of which are identical as the forecasts are not updated on 10 minute intervals.

To prune this data back, we carve it up into two sub-datasets:

- A *changed forecast* set, which contains rows of data where the forecast temperature for a date/time at a location has changed from the previous forecast.  
- A *day-lagged forecast* set, where we have rows where the time we requested the data was 1,2,3,...7 days from the forecast date/time.

The first set shows us how often the BOM updates their models, and the second we'll use to assess the accuracy of forecasts at certain durations prior to the forecast date and& time.  

```{r}
forecast_distinct |>
    ggplot() +
    geom_histogram(aes(hour(request_datetime_local_floored)), binwidth = 1) +
    scale_y_continuous(breaks = scales::breaks_pretty()) +
    labs(
        x = "Hour of Day",
        y = "Count"
    )
```


# Forecast Accuracy

Let's get to the crux of this article: forecast accuracy. There's a small fly in the ointment, in that the forecast temperatures that we've pulled from the BOM are integers, whereas the temperatures have a single decimal place. I assume BOMs forecast models output reals then they round up or down to the nearest whole number. So a decision: do we round the recorded temperatures to whole numbers as well, or keep them as reals? I've made the decision here to continue to use the decimal temperatures, my argument being I don't want to remove information. My assumption is that the BOM is rounding to the nearest whole number, but to me what they've published on their site is the model.

To start, here's a view of both the 1 day and 6 day forecast overlaid across the recorded temperature for one site (Essendon Airport in Melbourne). Initially it's a bit messy, but if you zoom in and add/remove the each of the forecasts you get an idea about how it's changed, and how far off each one is.

```{r}
dat <- 
forecast_lagged |>
    filter(wmo == 95866) 

plot_ly(data = dat |> filter(forecast_duration == days(6)) , x = ~forecast_datetime_local, y = ~temperature, type = 'scatter', name = 'Recorded Temperature', mode = 'lines+markers') |>
add_trace(data = dat |> filter(forecast_duration == days(6)) , y = ~forecast_temp, name = '6 Day Forecast', type = 'scatter', mode = 'lines+markers', alpha = .6) |> 
add_trace(data = dat |> filter(forecast_duration == days(1)) , y = ~forecast_temp, name = '1 Day Forecast', type = 'scatter', mode = 'lines+markers', alpha = .6) 
```



```{r echo=FALSE}
forecast_accuracy <- function(forecasts) {
    forecasts |> 
    mutate(temp_diff = forecast_temp - temperature)
}

forecast_statistics <- function(forecasts) {
    forecasts |> 
    summarise(
        mean_absolute_error = mean(abs(temp_diff)),
        mean_forecast_error = mean(temp_diff),
        sd_forecast_error = sd(temp_diff),
        nine_five_quantile = list(quantile(temp_diff, probs = c(.025, .975))),
        #excess_kurtosis = mean( ( (temp_diff - mean(temp_diff)) / sd(temp_diff))^4 ) - 3,
        #n = n()
        .groups = 'drop'
    ) 
}

forecast_lagged <- forecast_lagged |> forecast_accuracy()
forecast_distinct <- forecast_distinct |> forecast_accuracy()

forecast_lagged_summary  <- 
    forecast_lagged |> 
    group_by(forecast_duration) |>
    forecast_statistics()
```

Let's look at some statistics across all of the weather stations: the mean error, the mean absolutle error, and the standard deviation of the mean error.

```{r echo=FALSE}
forecast_lagged_summary |>
    mutate(forecast_duration = as.duration(forecast_duration)) |> 
    gt() |>
    cols_label(
        forecast_duration = "Forecast Duration",
        mean_forecast_error = "Mean Error",
        mean_absolute_error = "Mean Absolute Error",
        sd_forecast_error = "Standard Deviation of Error",
        nine_five_quantile = "95% Quantile"
    )
```

Looking at the mean error first, which doesn't tell us about the accuracy (as negative and positive errors cancel each other out), but more about the bias in the model. It's showing as that across all forecasts there's a slight bias towards over-estimating the temperature, but only by a tenth of a degree or so. The absolute error of is goes up from 1.33 degrees a day out, so 1.88 degrees six days out. The standard deviation increasing tells us that the errors are more spread out as well. None of this is unexpected.

A histogram of the errors shows their distribution and how that changes over time. I've overlaid a Gaussian with a fix mean of 0 and standard deviation of 2 to act as point of comparison:

```{r echo=FALSE}
forecast_lagged |>
    ggplot() +
    geom_histogram(aes(temp_diff, after_stat(density), fill = forecast_duration_names), binwidth = .1) +
    geom_function(fun = dnorm, args = list(mean = 0, sd = 2), alpha = .5) +
    facet_wrap(vars(forecast_duration_names)) +
    labs(
        fill = "Forecast Duration"
    ) +
    theme(legend.position = 'none') +
    labs(
      title = 'Histogram of Forecast Error per Forecast Duration',
      x = 'Forecast Error',
      y = 'Density'
    )
```
This shows the mean error slightly shifting to the right and the distribution spreading out as the standard deviation increases. The distribution looks *sort of* normal, and for the first two standard deviations it tracks really well. You'll notice this with the table above, where the mean +- 2 x std.dev lines up well with the 95% quartiles.

But we can't assume it's normal across the board. This is best visualised with a quantile-quantile (QQ) plot, which shows that these distrbutions have much fatter tails than a normal.

```{r echo=FALSE}
forecast_lagged |>
    ggplot(aes(sample = temp_diff, group = forecast_duration)) +
    geom_qq(size = .1, distribution = stats::qnorm) +
    geom_qq_line(distribution = stats::qnorm) +
    facet_wrap(vars(forecast_duration_names)) +
    scale_x_continuous(breaks = scales::breaks_pretty())
```
Instead of looking at whole days between the forecast and the recrorded temperature, let's use our look at the data set which contains all of the changed forecasts over time, therefore showing a wide spectrum of times between when the forecast was made, and the actual recorded temperature.
```{r}
forecast_distinct |>
    group_by(forecast_duration, state) |> 
    forecast_statistics() |> 
    ggplot() +
    geom_point(aes(forecast_duration, mean_absolute_error, colour = state), size = .5, alpha = .5) +
    facet_wrap(vars(state)) +
    scale_x_time() +
    labs(
        title = 'Mean Absolute Forecast Error by Forecast Duration',
        x = 'Hours',
        y = 'Mean Absolute Error',
        colour = 'State'
    ) +
    theme(legend.position = 'none')
```
There's two things that immediately jump out at me: the is first is both the variance and mean absolute error over time appear to be much greater for Victoria, Tasmania, and South Australia as opposed to the others. The other is that there appears to be a cyclical nature to the forecast error. Setting aside what I'll call the 'southern state' issue, we'll investigate the cyclical nature.

Straight away this looks to be a difference in forecast error based on whether it's day or night. I wouldn't have thought that this kind of bias would have come through in the forecast durations. But if you recall the histogram above that showed when the BOM updated its models, this wasn't uniformly distributed. Rather, most of the changes to forecasts ocurred between 2pm and 6pm. Therefore the forcast duration - the difference between the forecast change and the forecast time - will be 'tethered' from this time and the day/night cycle will show in the forecast durations.

We can code the forecast's time as either 'night' based on whether the time is after 7pm and before 7am, and 'day' for all others. This makes sense for the time period of the data. Interestingly if we look at a density plot of the forecast error we see a difference in variation between night and day.

```{r}
forecast_distinct <-
    forecast_distinct |>
    mutate(day_phase = if_else( hour(temp_datetime) >= 19 | hour(temp_datetime) <= 7, 'night', 'day'))
```


```{r}
forecast_distinct |>
    filter(state == 'VIC') |> 
    ggplot() +
    geom_histogram(aes(temp_diff, after_stat(density), fill = day_phase), binwidth = .2, position = 'stack') +
    facet_wrap(~day_phase) +
    labs(
        title = "Victoria - Day Phase Forecast Error",
        subtitle = "Histogram of Density, Night Defined as Between 19:00-7:00",
        x = "Forecast Error",
        y = "Density",
        fill = "Day Phase"
    ) +
    theme(legend.position = 'none')
```
This, combined with the fact that most of the forecasts are published at a certain time of the day, is why there is a cyclical pattern in the forecast duration graph. We won't delve into this any further, but the question to of course ask is why would this be the case?

# Forecast Error Modelling 

Can we find a way to model the forecast error as a function of some other parameters? The first parameter is obvious: forecast duration. Looking at the mean absolute error over the forecast duration, it increased as the forecast duration increased. This shouldn't be surprising, as the quote goes "prediction is very hard, especially about the future".

But when we looked at the graph, there was a second source of variation. It appeared that the southern states - Victoria, Tasmania, South Australia - had a higher variation in mean absolute error as compared to the northern states.

This is where I'm in danger of cosplaying as a meteorologist and being completely off the mark, but lets have a go. Looking back at the graph of the lowest mean temperature and the start of this article, taken from Tasmania in the south of Australia, what you may have noticed is that the profile of the lowest mean temperature looked different. To me eye it looked rougher and moved up and down in a far more random fashion as compared to the highest mean, which was from a location in the far north of Australia.

## Creating a Jaggedness Index

My guess would be as we go south, certain meteorological features that I would only be guessing at (weather coming off the Southern Ocean?) make the rises and falls in temperature less 'smooth' and more random, and thus more unpredictable.

How can we quantify this? What I have done is calculated a 'jaggedness' index for each of the locations and each of the day-lagged forecast. This is done by summing the absolute difference between successive temperature readings, then diving by the number of observations.

```{r}
jagged_data <-
    temp_data |> 
    group_by(wmo, state) |>
    summarise(
        jaggedness = sum(abs(diff(temperature)))/n(),

                .groups = 'drop'
    ) |>
    right_join(forecast_lagged, by = c('wmo', 'state')) |> 
    group_by(wmo, state, jaggedness, forecast_duration_names) |>
    summarise(
        mean_absolute_error = mean(abs(temp_diff)),
        .groups = 'drop'
    )
```

Plotting this on a graph, with an animation transitioning between each of the day forecast durations, we can see a reasonably linear relationship between this jaggedness index and the mean forecast error.

```{r message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
jagged_data |> 
    ggplot() +
    geom_point(aes(jaggedness, mean_absolute_error, colour = state), size = 2) +
    transition_states(forecast_duration_names, state_length = 6, transition_length = 12) +
    ease_aes('linear') +
    labs(
        title = 'Temperature Jaggedness versus Forecast Accuracy',
        subtitle = "Forecast Duration: {closest_state}",
        x = 'Temperature',
        y = 'Forecast Error',
        colour = 'State'
    ) 
```

Let's use these two features - duration and jaggedness - as parameters into a simple linear model.

## Modeling

We'll use Stan to create a simple linear model with two parameters: the jaggedness as a continuous variable, and the day-lagged forecast duration as a categorical index. Here's the specifical of the model, which is the only code I'll show in the entire post:

```{r}
fcast_error_mdl <- cmdstan_model('model.stan')
fcast_error_mdl
```

```{r include=FALSE}
fcast_error_fit <- fcast_error_mdl$sample(
    data = compose_data(
        jagged_data |> select(
            duration = forecast_duration_names,
            jaggedness,
            mean_forecast_error = mean_absolute_error
        )
    ),
    seed = 3249,
    chains = 4,
    parallel_chains = 4,
    step_size = 0.1,
    refresh = 500,
)
```

After sampling from the posterior (and performing some of the standard checks like trace plots and histograms that I'm not going to show here), we get our alpha and beta parameters (intercept and slope) for each of the indexes, where index 1 = 1 day forecast, index 2 = 2 day forecast, etc, as well as some other statistics about the distributions of each one. For completeleness here's a table of all of the parameters;


```{r message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
fcast_error_fit$summary(
    variables = c('sigma', 'alpha', 'beta_jaggedness'),
    c('mean', 'median', 'sd'),
    quantiles = ~ quantile(., probs = c(0.025, 0.975))
) |> 
    gt() |> 
    cols_label(
        variable = "Variable",
        mean = 'Mean',
        median = 'Median',
        sd = 'Std. Dev',
        '2.5%' = 'Lower 2.5%',
        '97.5%' = 'Upper 97.5'
    ) |>
    cols_align('center') |> 
    tab_style(
        style = cell_text(weight = 'bold'),
        locations = cells_column_labels()
    )
```

Taking a look at index *[6]* - the six day forecast - the parameters tell us the following information:

- *alpha[6]* - for a location with zero jaggedness (the intercept), you would expect on average a mean forecast error of 0.82, with a credible interval between .7 and .94.
- *beta_jaggedness[6]* - for each increase of jaggedness by 1, you would expect on average the mean forecast error to go up by 4 degrees, with a 95% credible interval of between 3.56 and 4.46. 

Tables are all well a

```{r}
fcast_error_fit |>
    spread_draws(alpha[i], beta_jaggedness[i]) |>
    mutate(i = as_factor(i)) |>
    mutate(i = fct_recode(i, '1 day' = '1', '2 day' = '2', '3 day' = '3', '4 day' = '4', '5 day' = '5', '6 day' = '6')) |> 
    ggplot() +
    geom_point(data = jagged_data |> rename(i = forecast_duration_names), aes(jaggedness, mean_absolute_error, colour = i)) +
    geom_abline(aes(intercept = alpha, slope = beta_jaggedness, colour = i), alpha = .01) +
    transition_states(i, state_length = 6, transition_length = 12) +
    ease_aes('linear')
```

```{r}
fcast_error_fit |>
    spread_draws(y_rep[i]) |>
    sample_frac(.2) |> 
    filter(
        y_rep < quantile(y_rep, .975) & y_rep > quantile(y_rep, .025)
    ) |> 
    group_by(i) |>
    nest() |>
    bind_cols(jagged_data) |>
    unnest(data) |>
    ggplot() +
    geom_point(aes(jaggedness, y_rep), alpha = .1, size = .1, colour = 'lightblue') +
    geom_point(aes(jaggedness, mean_absolute_error), size = .1) +
    facet_wrap(vars(forecast_duration_names))
```

```{r}
fcast_error_fit |>
    spread_draws(y_rep[i]) |>
    sample_frac(.5) |> 
    group_by(i) |>
    nest() |>
    bind_cols(jagged_data) |>
    unnest(data) |>
    mutate(q = mean_absolute_error > quantile(y_rep, .025) & mean_absolute_error < quantile(y_rep, .975)) |>
    group_by(forecast_duration_names) |> 
    summarise(q95 = sum(q)/n()) |>
    gt()
```

